1
00:00:02,140 --> 00:00:04,240
J: Come on Marco.

2
00:00:04,240 --> 00:00:08,320
We’ve got to get on and sort out this project for Professor Barclay.

3
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:09,560
M: Hang on.

4
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:14,800
I want to make sure we’ve got all the information.

5
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:17,059
Now …… where are
we?

6
00:00:17,059 --> 00:00:24,739
J: Well, today we need to sort out exactly what we’re going to do and how we’re
going to divide the work up.

7
00:00:24,739 --> 00:00:25,100
M: OK.

8
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:28,679
How long have we got, by the way?

9
00:00:28,679 --> 00:00:45,259
J: Um… the end of term is April 6th and he said to hand it in on week 8, so that’s
March 25th at the latest because the beginning of that week is the 21st.

10
00:00:45,259 --> 00:00:45,920
So not long!

11
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:47,079
M: Right.

12
00:00:47,640 --> 00:00:48,960
Have you got the notes there?

13
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:58,719
J: Yes, he wants us to do a fairly small-scale study, like the last one, on whether or not
businesses were offering more benefits to staff.

14
00:00:58,719 --> 00:01:04,159
We’ve now got to look at the rise in
older workers.

15
00:01:04,159 --> 00:01:05,019
Should be fairly straightforward.

16
00:01:05,019 --> 00:01:09,680
M: Yeah, as long as we keep it small.

17
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:10,319
Who’s marking it?

18
00:01:10,319 --> 00:01:17,060
J: Don’t know – sometimes he gets the PhD students to mark it for him.

19
00:01:17,659 --> 00:01:20,359
M: Oh actually it just says here ‘a senior lecturer’.

20
00:01:20,359 --> 00:01:25,299
I suppose it’s too much for
Professor Barclay to do them all.

21
00:01:25,659 --> 00:01:27,019
J: Yeah.

22
00:01:27,019 --> 00:01:29,060
Anyway, how are we going to go about this?

23
00:01:29,060 --> 00:01:39,959
M: Well, we have to decide how big we want it to be and who we’re…
J: … Yeah, but I think we must sort out a timetable for the project otherwise nothing
will get done.

24
00:01:39,959 --> 00:01:40,359
M: OK.

25
00:01:40,359 --> 00:01:45,500
Do you want to do that?

26
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:45,739
J: Alright.

27
00:01:45,739 --> 00:01:48,459
I'll do it as soon as we finish here.

28
00:01:48,459 --> 00:01:53,900
M: OK – what do we have to do now for the project?

29
00:01:53,900 --> 00:01:56,599
What’s the best way to go about
it?

30
00:01:56,599 --> 00:02:04,060
Um… well, Professor Carter suggested we set up a focus group to get some
in-depth interviews but I think that’ll take a lot of time.

31
00:02:04,060 --> 00:02:05,420
J: Yeah, I agree.

32
00:02:05,420 --> 00:02:13,680
If we did a focus group, we’d have to spend time deciding who to
include in it and it’s not necessary to do one anyway.

33
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:14,560
M: Oh,

34
00:02:14,560 --> 00:02:28,860
J: And, if you agree, I think we should get in touch with the businesses on the list
Professor Carter gave us and ask them if they’re prepared to participate.

35
00:02:28,860 --> 00:02:33,319
M: Sounds good – then we can go there, give them questionnaires and collect them
later.

36
00:02:33,319 --> 00:02:33,759
J: Exactly.

37
00:02:33,759 --> 00:02:34,419
M: OK.

38
00:02:35,099 --> 00:02:41,900
Then do we need to book one of those study rooms in the library so we can
work together to input the data?

39
00:02:41,900 --> 00:02:48,300
Perhaps not, as I guess just one of us could just sort
it out, actually.

40
00:02:48,300 --> 00:02:51,300
J: Yes, that would be easier.

41
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:55,599
A lot of what we’re doing is qualitative, so it’ll be writing
up rather than statistics.

42
00:02:55,599 --> 00:02:57,340
No software for that I’m afraid!

43
00:02:57,340 --> 00:03:08,580
,
M: And I think it would look better if we had actual shots of some of the staff because
we’re citing appearance as a factor in employability, aren’t we?

44
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:09,080
J: Yeah.

45
00:03:09,599 --> 00:03:09,799
OK.

46
00:03:09,780 --> 00:03:12,400
I’ll factor that all in when I sort everything out tonight.

47
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:14,539
M: I’m glad we decided to work together.

48
00:03:14,539 --> 00:03:18,340
I think it’s going to work out well.

49
00:03:18,340 --> 00:03:26,939
J: Yes, well, given that we had to work in pairs on this project, I think we were right to
choose each other.

50
00:03:26,939 --> 00:03:32,340
We complement each other academically as we’re each good at
what the other isn’t!

51
00:03:32,340 --> 00:03:36,020
In fact, we should have tried working together before!

52
00:03:36,139 --> 00:03:37,060
M: Yes!

53
00:03:37,060 --> 00:03:39,520
Now, how shall we split the work?

54
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:41,939
I’ll do the analysis, shall I?

55
00:03:41,939 --> 00:03:43,300
J: Oh – OK.

56
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:52,020
M: It’s just that it might be faster because I’m used to doing it – although your English
is better than mine.

57
00:03:52,020 --> 00:03:54,199
I need more practice at reading, really.

58
00:03:54,199 --> 00:03:54,879
J: OK.

59
00:03:54,879 --> 00:03:56,460
I’ll do the presentation then.

60
00:03:56,460 --> 00:03:58,419
If that’s OK with you?

61
00:03:58,419 --> 00:03:58,960
M: Yeah, sure.

62
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:00,680
I don’t mind speaking in public but I hate preparing all the notes for
them.

63
00:04:00,680 --> 00:04:00,879
J: Thing is, the tutor said one person should do the whole presentation and he said he
expected me to do it because I haven’t done one yet.

64
00:04:00,879 --> 00:04:01,079
M: No, that’s fine.

65
00:04:01,079 --> 00:04:01,279
Now…

